
// draft
flatten(list_of_lists: Int[][]) -> Int[]
	note "join the inner lists end to end into one list, removing one level of nesting and keeping every element in its place"
	# The output has exactly the elements of the inner lists taken together, each as many times as
	# it occurs across them.
	// reaching for: out same_elements_as the built-in flatten applied to list_of_lists. This
	// obligation is itself named flatten, and a property that calls its own obligation runs the
	// performance under test, so the line would compare out with itself and claim nothing. I want a
	// way to name the built-in, or an operator that concatenates a list of lists, so that out's
	// multiset of values can be compared with the inner lists' multiset taken together.
	# The elements of each inner list appear in the output in the same order as in that inner list.
	all l in list_of_lists: l same_order_as out
	# Every element of an inner list comes before every element of each later inner list in the
	# output.
	// reaching for: for each pair of inner lists a and b with a earlier in list_of_lists, each
	// element x of a sits at an earlier position in out than each element y of b, where "position"
	// means the occurrence in out that x and y themselves became. Writing "x before y in out"
	// places x and y at the first occurrence of their values in out, because they are not
	// occurrences of out. That makes the line false when a value repeats: on [[1, 2], [2, 1]] the
	// correct output [1, 2, 2, 1] fails, since 2's first occurrence is after 1's. I want to say
	// that an element of an inner list lands in out at the summed lengths of the earlier inner
	// lists plus its own position in that list, and the notation has no index binder to say it.
	// example wanted: the docstring's [[0, 1], [2, 3]]: the inner lists are joined end to end
	// example wanted: an empty outer list []: there are no inner lists, so there are no elements to
	// keep
	// example wanted: empty inner lists among non-empty ones, [[], [1], []]: an empty inner list
	// adds nothing and takes no position
	// example wanted: the same value in two inner lists, [[1, 2], [2, 1]]: both copies of each
	// value are kept, and the order across lists decides where each copy goes
	// example wanted: a single inner list [[3, 1, 2]]: the output keeps its order and does not sort
	// it
